  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1241 AM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nCAMS indicate the area of rain showers and weak storms (outside of \nthe severe storm along the White River earlier) will continue to \nprogress east through the early morning hours, departing the eastern \nCWA around 12Z. A ridge builds overhead today, with increasing \nheights with and upstream wave that will move from Montana into \nNorth Dakota tonight. NAM BUFKIT profiles indicate daytime heating \ncould generate some skinny CAPE, and CAMS generate a stippling of \nQPF, but without an upper wave and high clouds spilling over the \nridge, suspect anything that is generated will rapidly dissipate and \nas such will stick with NBM POPs (about 5-10%). \n\nThe wave out west will present us with another round of moisture \nchances tonight, but in the form of light showers with NAM BUFKIT \nindicating profiles are moist adiabatic. NBM POPs are only around 20-\n40% with only a few hundreds QPF. \n\nIn regards to smoke, With the source regions out west, smoke aloft is \na given though the brief transition of an upper ridge overhead \nFriday means a trajectory of air aloft out of 4-Corners region which \nhas fewer/less widespread fires. While the RAP shows some surface \nconcentrations, widespread rainfall should mitigate this. Persistent \neasterly low level flow for Today and southeasterlies for Friday \nmeans smoke free air is advecting in the low levels. As such, \nremoved any mention of smoke as a surface obscuration.  \n\nA southwest flow regime sets up for Friday-Saturday. Most of the \nmoisture ends up in North Dakota, with a weak trough passage across \nSouth Dakota. This is a minor wind shift with little appreciable \nweather. Will have to check to see if there is any real threat of \nsmoke in this post system airmass, but at this point we are on the \neastern side of the high in northwest flow and a source region \ncloser to northern Canada than western Canada and the Pacific \nNorthwest so probably won't be an issue regardless.",
